# Round v41l01: V4.1's fused card layer reads a Q5_K down, so layers 0 and 1 (Q3_K gate and up, Q5_K
# down) take card experts. No kernel moves (narrow-scan b96cfcc0..26d8982b: four scan bins identical).
# Plan on the A6000, place a, P = 512, the plan room (generate_ds41 --plan): base 1326 card experts,
# n_l 34..35 on 38 layers; head 1307, n_l 32..33 on 40 layers. So layers 0 and 1 gain about 32 each and
# layers 2..39 give up about 2 each.
# Derivation [derived], from round v41split's step records (rig-log via #1162 comment 6626463373):
# layers 0 and 1's host leg is 1.7-1.9 ms a step, fully exposed; their 200 hottest experts take
# 66-79 % of their hits, so about 32 a layer take 35-50 % [assumption, the concave curve below 100]:
# +0.60..+0.95 ms. The 76 experts the other layers give up are near-uniform (6 of 384 a token):
# 1.19 more host slots a token x 135-145 us = -0.16..-0.20 ms. Net +0.40..+0.79 ms of a 21.8 ms
# (prose) / 19.5 ms (code) host round: +1.8..+4.0 %.
kind: ab
question: does carding V4.1's layers 0 and 1 (the fused layer with a Q5_K down) shorten the decode step
  by the exposed host leg those layers' card experts capture, less the slots the other layers give up?
term: decode tok/s @ depth 512 (the corpora's first 512 ids), prose and code, A6000, plan (a) - (v41l01 - base) / base, paired
  by round
unit: %
predict: +1.8..+4.0
rounds: 6
sd: 0.6 % AGENTS.md "Know the ruler": same-binary runs scatter with SD 0.6 %
condition: generate_ds41 of v41l01 and of b96cfcc05 (built in a base worktree before the lease, a bin:
  arm) alternate in one lease on the A6000, BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=6 just depth-gpu-ds41 with the prose
  and code corpora at P = 512 (the step records' prompt) and a bin: base arm each, the order rotated every round,
  BLOOMERY_STEP_STATS=1 on both so the host leg and host slots print per arm; no row carries
  [cpu-busy] or [other-busy]. The pp rows at P = 512 and 4096 are read as no-regression beside it
decide-in: land v41l01 with the regenerated V4.1 fixture; re-derive #1162's Spark gain on the card
  layers only
decide-below: read the step records: layers 0 and 1's host slots per token on both arms; if they did
  not fall, the residency or the plan did not put the hot experts there, and the plan's per-layer
  pick is the next round, not the kernel
decide-above: the other layers' loss is smaller than near-uniform (their marginal experts are cold),
  so the per-layer split itself is a lever: write it into the placement model before #1162
